  "facts": [
    "PyString is sealed; its typed equality only compares owned UTF-8 spans. There is no callback, allocation, frame, structural work or cancellation checkpoint.",
    "Hashing is unchanged and hashes the same UTF-8 bytes. Identity remains true for this exact pair only.",
    "Ordinary PyEquality keeps numeric rules before identity, preserving NaN value non-reflexivity. Key probing is different: identical non-null keys must match even for non-reflexive hashable scalars.",
    "TryAsString also handles IPyStringCoercibleValue. Do not widen the proposed exact pair to those values.",
    "The generic comparer suppression remains required for mixed, protocol and structural keys, including R13 reentrant comparisons. Exact strings cannot observe the temporarily incremented suppression depth."
  ],
  "replacement": "First accept identical non-null keys; next compare exact PyString pairs through typed UTF-8 equality; preserve the original suppression/equality fallback and hash method.",
  "perPairOmissions": "Comparer suppression scope/thread-static traffic and general numeric/structural dispatch. No global mechanism is retired because the remaining types require it.",
  "contractTradeoff": "Correct existing NaN-key lookup/updates/membership. Ordinary value equality remains unchanged. General distinct pairs pay an extra identity/type guard; native timings remain diagnostic only.",
  "stoppingRule": "One native baseline/candidate, focused semantics/owner/fuel/goldens, one existing complete JSON-helper capture. No new trace/framework, variants, new state/ownership layer or general equality reordering. Revert if semantic proof fails or general controls materially regress.",
